    {
      "type": "LinguisticObject",
      "content": "The artist; sold to Galerie Bernheim-Jeune, Paris, 1907 [this and the following according to Ducrey 2005]. Pierre Etienne Goujon (1875–1914) and Lily Goujon (née Reinach, 1885–1971), before 1914; their sale, Hotel Drouot, Paris, Mar. 28, 1919, lot 71bis (hors catalogue). Possibly Galerie Druet, Paris [according to London 1967 and email correspondence with Marina Ducrey, Nov. 29, 2018, in curatorial object file]. Mrs. R. G. Wood, by 1967; sold, Sotheby's, London, Nov. 29, 1967 to Walter Feilchenfeldt, Zurich; sold to Mary Runnells (died 1977), 1968 [letter from Mrs. Walter Feilchenfeldt to Mary Runnells, Oct. 2, 1968, in curatorial object file]; bequeathed to the Art Institute of Chicago, 1977.",
      "classified_as": [
        {
          "id": "http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435438",
          "type": "Type",
          "_label": "Provenance Statement",
          "classified_as": [
            {
              "id": "http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300418049",
              "type": "Type",
              "_label": "Brief Text"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "LinguisticObject",
      "content": "Inspired by a personal photograph, this work reflects Vallotton's focus on the daily life of his household. This example employs bold blocks of solid color: the red of the bed and the pink of the child's dress, for example. The result is a graphic simplicity similar to the artist's woodblock prints of interior scenes created during this same period.",
      "classified_as": [
        {
          "id": "http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026032",
          "type": "Type",
          "_label": "Summary",
          "classified_as": [
